congested, and I will

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.980
try not to cough into the mic.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.670
And I'll speak slowly
and as loudly as I can.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:25.440
So I wanted to show you or
talk about the breadth of what

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.630
Connect can do and
support because it

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.100
is getting quite complex.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.310
And that's one of my favorite
things to do at conference

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:37.230
is talk about what you're
doing with Connect,

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.840
what workflows
you're building out,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.420
what things you're creating.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:48.350
And this diagram
just represents some

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.300
of what those things could be.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.350
But interacting with Connect
should be quite simple.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.850
It shouldn't be complex.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.830
Essentially, what
you're doing is creating

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.170
something in R or Python.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.150
You're publishing
that thing to Connect,

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.030
and then you're letting
Connect take over it,

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.150
executing that thing
for you and whoever

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.380
else you share that thing
with, this is it on Connect.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.540
Under the hood,
Connect allows you

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.880
to do some really cool stuff.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.900
You can run multiple
versions of R and Python.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:24.340
You can now run multiple
versions of Quarto as well

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.880
on the single server.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.000
And your applications,
your documents, your APIs,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.850
everything that you create
and publish to Connect--

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.190
all of those things
are sandboxed apart

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.110
from one another
so that you can run

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.120
those different
versions of packages

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.440
for all of your content.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.640
Fundamentally, though, if
you're using Connect today,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.690
all of the content you
publish there, all of it

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:54.300
runs using these local processes
on the same host or container

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.155
as the Connect server.

00:01:56.155 --> 00:01:58.030
And that's what I want
to draw your attention

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:02.770
to because this talk is what
comes next for Connect here.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:07.210
It's about our next evolution
for content execution

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.972
and our story there.

00:02:09.972 --> 00:02:13.620
So today we're really excited
to announce Off-Host Content

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.010
Execution for Connect
on Kubernetes.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.840
This feature will
be in beta for now.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.750
But if you are a
Connect admin, we

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.552
would love for you
to give it a trial.

00:02:24.552 --> 00:02:26.160
What beta means?

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.970
In a bit, let's talk about
what this means for you.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.200
So in this world, when you're
running Off-Host Content

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.280
Execution, now instead of
using local processes to run

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.040
all of your content items in
the same place as the Connect

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.630
server, Kubernetes is used
to execute your content.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.360
And all of your jobs are
external to the container

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.030
that's running that
Connect server.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.780
So I want to pause
here and make sure

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:58.840
that if you are a data
scientist or a Connect

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.870
publisher in the
audience today, you

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.660
should know that I am not
here to tell you that you need

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.800
to run home and learn how to
containerize your applications

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.990
or teach yourself Kubernetes.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:17.170
Our goal is not to take away the
simplicity of that publishing

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.470
user experience like I
talked about at the beginning

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:22.580
of the presentation.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.370
In fact, the publishing
user experience

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:29.720
should not change remarkably
so if you are running

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.350
Off-Host Content Execution.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.640
So instead of telling
you, let me show you

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.930
what that looks like.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.130
If you come over here to
the Documentation page

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.840
in the Connect
dashboard, and you

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.410
scroll down to where you used
to see all of the versions of R,

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.190
Python, Quarto that are
available on your server,

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.960
if you're running
off-host executions,

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:58.050
you'll now see a list or
a set of execution images.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.170
And these are going to
show you the options

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:05.070
that you have as a
publisher for what

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:08.520
sorts of environments that can
be used to build and execute

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.640
your content.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.510
You can choose to target a
specific one of these images

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:19.100
if you're publishing, or you
can let Connect just pick for you.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:20.839
It has a version
mapping algorithm,

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:23.810
just like it picks for you
when you do publishing today.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.722
Can you still hear me?

00:04:33.722 --> 00:04:35.180
I guess, nobody's
