00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.840
So when I open it
up here, I will

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.420
be able to see a content item.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.600
And it will use that
same execution image.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.840
Now, you will notice
a little bit of delay

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.620
here when you launch an
application in Off-Host

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.360
Execution mode.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.770
And some of that has to do maybe
sometimes with pulling down

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.300
that execution image.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.270
But a lot of the
cost is wrapped up

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.582
in job spawning and
process startup.

00:06:14.582 --> 00:06:16.290
So there is a little
bit more of a delay.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:20.160
But again, if you wanted
to work around that,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.970
you have the same tools as
you do with local execution

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.970
where you can set the
minimum processes.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.280
You want to have
one always available

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.800
if this is a particularly
important app that

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.880
needs to come up very
quickly for your users.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.170
Now let's see which
content execution image

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:41.600
Connect actually picked for me.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:46.850
And I'll jump up
here so you can see.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:47.700
Scroll down.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.670
You'll see you have a
new piece of metadata

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:52.550
here at the bottom of
the Content Info pane.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:56.360
And this just lists
the image that got

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:00.010
used for our last execution.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.010
If I wasn't intending
to target that image,

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:07.600
then I would need to go back
and regenerate the manifest file

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.050
and specify a new
