00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:36.650
to maybe the Administrator
persona about what

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:38.890
this looks like.

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:43.720
Let's talk about off-host
execution from that angle.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.100
Now, if you are an
admin, you should

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:52.680
know that this functionality
uses the same components

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.920
if you're familiar with
the RStudio Workbench

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.180
product and its new
Job Launcher component.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:05.200
We're using that same paradigm
but bundling it with that.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.280
So if you want to run off-host
execution with Connect,

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.970
you're going to need to use
a version of Connect that

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:14.110
has the Job Launcher
bundled with it.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:17.400
So recommend starting with
the latest release of Connect,

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.700
which is our July edition
that went out last week.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.610
In addition to that, you're
going to need to have,

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:27.750
as always, a valid Connect
license and then database.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:30.480
And that will be
Postgres for this case.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.180
And then some shared
file storage--

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:38.340
could be NFS, or EFS,
anything that's ReadWriteMany.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.990
On the Kubernetes requirement
side, you're going to need,

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:43.320
of course, a working
Kubernetes cluster.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:47.010
You're going to need the
Kubernetes command line tool,

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:54.000
and also Helm version 3,
which is the package manager

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:56.660
for Kubernetes in this case.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:59.540
If you have all of those
things, you're basically

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:01.290
set up to go at this point.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.650
And we have
documentation for how

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:08.270
to run through, install,
and configure to get

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:10.130
all the way to this point.

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:13.670
But I've showed a lot
of architecture diagrams

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:17.840
at this point, and
